[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
**Level 2 of 4 - System Design Concept.**

The overall technical architecture: the system's structure, its components and
their interactions, before diving into detailed element designs. Written for
designers and developers while remaining accessible to non-technical
stakeholders.

**Scope boundaries.** L1 provides the business context this document
implements. L3 gives the detailed specification per software element. Deployment
architecture, infrastructure specification, integration configuration and
technical implementation detail belong in a **System Realization Concept** - not
here. When a description starts naming processor counts or connection pool
sizes, it has left this level.

**Document evolution.** Created early to establish the architecture. References
to element designs accumulate as those are written. Goals, quality requirements
and constraints get refined as implementation detail emerges. Keep the
architecture diagram current - a stale diagram is worse than none.
<<<

[[SECTION]]
UID: L2-SG
TITLE: System goals

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
What the technical system shall achieve as part of the solution. These detail
the vision in terms technical people can act on. Focus on the *what* and *why*
at system level, never the *how* - that is system scenarios and element designs.

**Every system goal should have at least one system scenario demonstrating how
the system achieves it.** A goal with no scenario is either untested or
unnecessary.
<<<

[SYSTEM_GOAL]
UID: SG-01
OBLIGATION: shall
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Draft
TITLE: TBD
STATEMENT: >>>
TBD - What the system should achieve and why it matters, at system level.
<<<
SUCCESS_CRITERIA: >>>
TBD - *Optional.* Measurable or observable criteria indicating the goal is
achieved.

**Quantitative** where the goal is time-based (speed, duration, response time),
volume-based (users, transactions, data items), quality-metricated (success
rate, error rate, uptime) or efficiency-based (steps required, resources used).

**Qualitative** where the goal is a subjective user experience, a binary
capability ("system can do X"), an integration ("integrates with Y") or a
completeness claim ("all data types supported").
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
TBD - *Optional.* Which higher-level vision or value proposition from L1 this
supports.
<<<
