[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: L2-SQR
TITLE: Quality requirements

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
How well the *entire system* performs - not individual elements, which is L3.
This is where a business quality requirement from L1 becomes a number.

Set `EXTERNALLY_SOURCED: Yes` where the requirement comes from a regulation,
standard or platform rather than from a business quality requirement. Such
requirements legitimately have no L1 parent, and marking them distinguishes
them from ones where the link was simply forgotten.
<<<

[SYSTEM_QUALITY_REQUIREMENT]
UID: SQR-01
QUALITY_CATEGORY: Performance
OBLIGATION: shall
PRIORITY: Must
STATUS: Draft
VERIFICATION: Test
EXTERNALLY_SOURCED: No
TITLE: TBD
STATEMENT: >>>
TBD - What aspect of system-wide quality is specified and why it matters.

Categories: `Performance` response times, throughput, resource usage;
`Scalability` handling growing workload; `Availability` uptime, reliability,
fault tolerance; `Security` authentication, authorisation, data protection;
`Usability` ease of use, learnability, accessibility; `Maintainability`
system-wide code quality, documentation, testability; `Compatibility` platform,
browser and integration support; `Data-integrity` consistency, accuracy,
validation; `Compliance` regulatory and standards adherence.
<<<
APPLIES_TO: >>>
TBD - The elements this primarily affects: [LINK: SE-01], [LINK: HE-01],
[LINK: PE-01], [LINK: UT-01], or the overall system architecture.
<<<
ACCEPTANCE_CRITERIA: >>>
TBD - Clear, testable, unambiguous.

**Quantitative** where performance, availability, scalability, security,
numerical standards or resource usage can be measured:
- "End-to-end completion within 30 seconds for 95% of requests"
- "Supports 1000 concurrent users without degradation"
- "99.5% uptime during operating hours"
- "All network communication uses TLS 1.2 or higher"

**Qualitative** where assessment is subjective, compliance is binary, or a
practice is required:
- "All interfaces follow platform-specific design guidelines"
- "System degrades gracefully when partner services are unavailable"
- "All sensitive data encrypted at rest and in transit"
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
TBD - *Optional.*
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: BQR-01
  ROLE: Supports

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: L2-SC
TITLE: Constraints

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Non-negotiable requirements limiting design and implementation choices for the
system as a whole, arriving from outside - laws, regulations, standards,
existing infrastructure, business decisions.

Many system constraints come from external sources and have no corresponding
business constraint. That is normal and expected; mark them
`EXTERNALLY_SOURCED: Yes` rather than inventing a business parent.
<<<

[SYSTEM_CONSTRAINT]
UID: SC-01
CONSTRAINT_CATEGORY: Technical
STATUS: Draft
SOURCE: TBD
EXTERNALLY_SOURCED: No
TITLE: TBD
STATEMENT: >>>
TBD - What the constraint requires or restricts, why it exists, and where it
comes from.

Categories: `Legal-regulatory` laws, regulations, compliance;
`Technical` technology choices, platform limits, existing infrastructure;
`Business` budget, timeline, organisational policy;
`Integration` requirements from partner or institutional systems;
`Standards` industry or company standards that must be followed.
<<<
APPLIES_TO: >>>
TBD - Which elements are affected: [LINK: SE-01], [LINK: HE-01],
[LINK: PE-01], or the overall system architecture.
<<<
ACCEPTANCE_CRITERIA: >>>
TBD - What must be done to satisfy the constraint.

**Quantitative** where there are versions, limits, dates or amounts:
- "All user-facing applications support iOS 14+ and Android 8+"
- "Personal data retention limited to 24 months"

**Qualitative** where compliance is binary or a technology is mandated:
- "All personal data handling complies with GDPR Articles 5-11"
- "Uses institutional SAML 2.0 authentication exclusively"
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
TBD - *Optional.*
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
TBD - What this rules out. A constraint whose consequence is unstated tends to
get ignored.
<<<
